ROADMAP.md:158:| B. Cumulative slices 0 and 1, then LP0 | Slice 0 establishes the running proof infrastructure: a closed `u8` arithmetic subset through every used IR to two unverified native builds, with per-lowering proofs and the composed conditional theorem. Then `ConcreteSpec` and the new compiler for slice 1 (the approved linear component, row Q8.2), on the same path; then LP0. See [vertical slices](#vertical-slices). | Each slice meets the [slice shape](#vertical-slices). Every judgment, semantics and pass is total. Fuel exhaustion is distinct from divergence. The native builds are qualified by tests, not proved: the LLVM output is labelled unverified, and its assumptions are listed with the lexer, model-translation and native-image obligations; a native-image claim, if any, is stated separately as conditional on those assumptions. Minimal provenance accompanies the first authoritative proof claims. The outside-reader test passes on the Lean rules. Phase B closes after slice 1, LP0 and B2/B3 acceptance; `legacy/` stays until LD. |
ROADMAP.md:161:| E. Verified growth | Proof-order items 4–8, the Lean Compiler Backend native path, the verified parser, typed contracts, frame reasoning, termination proofs and the proof library. Earlier slices already contain example-specific program theorems; E expands reusable models and proof support. Contracts have no source `old` and no snapshot construct (OD117): a postcondition reaches the entry state only through an explicit Lean theorem. Spec functions are total (OD115). An ordinary function can get its own termination proof, and there is no total-function category (OD116). | Each item's theorem holds, with its residual assumptions stated. |
ROADMAP.md:172:| T0 | Prepare the restart beside the old tree (decision row P7, approved 2026-11-05) | R-0568, R-0564 | Checklist, each item on main before T1: (1) the new root's CI jobs; (2) `required-checks.txt`, enforced by `train.sh watch` and `train.sh land`; (3) the required-check transition mechanism, an explicit old-to-new job mapping that is validated and needs the replacement checks to pass at the exact candidate SHA, never marking a dropped legacy check passed; (4) `ISSUES.tsv` and its validator; (5) `RETENTION.tsv` and the task disposition table; (6) `scripts/oracle/ORACLE.lock` with the oracle release artifact and its digest; (7) the archive records of the stopped services. |
ROADMAP.md:173:| T1 | Rehearse the move on the candidate root | R-0563 | On a throwaway branch holding the exact proposed day-one root, a fresh clone builds the candidate, hooks, runs exact-SHA CI and passes a dry-run landing, and the negative controls are refused for the expected reason. |
ROADMAP.md:174:| T2 | Move the old tree to `legacy/` in one train, after T0 and the T1 rehearsal | R-0558, R-0565 | The root holds only the day-one set. `legacy/` is byte-frozen and checked. The move itself lands on both remotes. The website is down. |
ROADMAP.md:175:| A1 | Dispose of the spec audit and draft the boundaries Core must satisfy | R-0555, R-0312, R-0316 | Each audit finding, gap and duplication has an owner and an acceptance artifact. The [boundary drafts](legacy/docs/compiler/A1_BOUNDARIES.md) exist. |
ROADMAP.md:176:| A2b | Formalize the approved design in a proposed `Spec.lean` (current work) | R-0511 | Exit to A2i: `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean`, aligned with the decided rows, is headed "PROPOSED: not normative until Core freeze"; the four design theorems are kernel-checked. The four design theorems (`reset_fresh`, `defer_cleanup_releases`, `open_mut_freezes_root`, `rep_roundtrip`) and their full obligations are kernel-checked without `native_decide`. Every row the formalization changes returns to the owner. This is separate from slice 1's soundness theorems. |
ROADMAP.md:177:| A2i | Integrate the proposed spec before the freeze ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06)) | R-0571, R-0574, R-0496, R-0560, R-0555, R-0559, R-0588, R-0592, R-0602, R-0603 | The separately proved parts share Core interfaces and one integrated semantics. Interaction proofs hold for a borrow across deferred cleanup, an allocator reset with outstanding loans and cleanup that needs authority, and the README obligations [O7 and O9](ConcreteSpec/README.md) are discharged. The semantic contract is stated. The normative definitions are one library in cohesive modules with `Spec.lean` as the public import. The browsable owner view exists. The pre-freeze part of R-0574 (no hidden traps and bounded execution) is in the proposed spec. An outside reader has read the integrated spec through that view. |
ROADMAP.md:178:| A2c | Core freeze | R-0567 | The owner approves the freeze of the integrated scopes after A2b's four theorems, A2i's integration proofs and an outside-reader pass. Only then is PROPOSED removed. A3 sends Core back only as a recovery when it exposes a substantive change. |
ROADMAP.md:179:| A3 | State the theorem and the trusted base in Lean | R-0502, R-0506, R-0508, R-0509, R-0500, R-0588 | The contract and the modular layout from A2i are on main. The theorem, with its holes, the named values-to-bytes boundary and the trusted-base inventory, is on main. The theorem skeleton uses concrete slice interfaces; its semantic-premise inventory is separate from the axiom audit. Before slice 0's authoritative claim, its actual Core fragment is connected to the frozen definitions and any instrumentation erasure it uses is proved; later features require integration proofs into that same Core. Slice 0 starts after A2c and the applicable A3 foundations for its authoritative claims; a provisional skeleton may start earlier and claims nothing normative. |
