ROADMAP.md:398:| R-0521 | Minimal provenance and one canonical verification task for the first authoritative claims | a claim names its compiler, source, theorem and executable digest; minimal provenance and kernel-checked per-compilation evidence accompany the first authoritative slice-0 claim; the evidence connects the exact input to the exact emitted BIR without trusting native evaluation, the backend input is bound to that BIR, and the Lean compiler, runtime and toolchain are listed as execution assumptions wherever the evidence does not close the gap (2026-11-06) | R-0509 |
ROADMAP.md:399:| R-0552 | Per-compilation certificates (owner-approved 2026-10-25) | a build emits a certificate per output, and the kernel checks it; minimal provenance and kernel-checked per-compilation evidence accompany the first authoritative slice-0 claim; each certificate connects the exact input to the exact emitted BIR through proved judgments or validators, checked by the kernel without trusting native evaluation; the backend input is bound to that BIR (a digest is identity, not correspondence); residual Lean compiler, runtime and toolchain trust is listed as explicit execution assumptions; LCB verification of compiled programs is not counted as verification of the compiler's execution (2026-11-06) | R-0504 |
ROADMAP.md:400:| R-0582 | Per-pass certificates in place of the whole-compiler `decide` certificate | after slice 0, a per-compilation certificate composes universal per-pass theorems with small per-edge witnesses, linear in program size, instead of making the kernel re-run the whole compiler by reduction; slice 0 may keep `decide`; a planted wrong witness at any edge is rejected | R-0552, R-0583 |
ROADMAP.md:401:| R-0528 | Resource limits on input size, memory, time and import closure | each input class has a limit and a hostile fixture | R-0503 |
ROADMAP.md:402:| R-0573 | Small adversarial interaction workloads from the first slices on, alongside file-copy (decided 2026-11-06) | each slice adds programs that combine its features with earlier ones in difficult ways (for example borrows across cleanup, authority in cleanup, nested views), each with its expected outcome and Lean/native differential check | R-0425, R-0503 |
ROADMAP.md:403:| R-0575 | The per-function facts report and query from slice 0 ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-facts-report), 2026-11-06) | every function of each slice has a report derived from the Lean rules, with a versioned schema: capabilities and the grants and resources actually reachable, allocation and allocator, trusted, foreign and opaque boundaries with their assumptions, each operation that could fail and how it is handled, termination (evidence with its named premises, or the responsible source permission and the call path to it), recursion depth and stack bound or why none is established, proof status per obligation, and completeness, each with its reason, evidence and source location; a permission that justifies nothing is reported as a fact, which a `Concrete.toml` policy may refuse (the decided refusals of an unmatched `#[proof]` and a duplicate `#[may_not_terminate]` stay) (carried from slice 0 into CheckedCore and the schema, spec obligations O1 and O4); the schema already has fields for what memory a function writes (frames) and what its outputs depend on (dependency flow), reported "not established" until derived: definite, maybe and never writes per `&mut` place and the output-to-input dependencies (control dependence, cleanup and opaque calls included) are derived into these fields as soon as `&mut` lands, not in E–G, while R-0524, R-0473 and R-0383 keep source frames, contracts and package summaries; facts are preserved across indirect calls, specialization and package boundaries (R-0588); from slice 0 the compiler answers a query about one function or module from the same facts, as readable text and in the machine format (the command is `concrete facts`); the report never claims what the rules do not establish; every fact is a typed judgment in Lean with a derivation-soundness theorem (and obligation-discovery completeness where it claims absence), one derivation feeds the readable view, the machine format and every policy, each fact has a closed status (established, conditional, refuted, not established, not applicable) with per-fact coverage, unknown never satisfies a policy, and answers carry typed identities, versioned schema and semantics and artifact digests; from slice 0 the report also carries the build identity (compiler SHA, toolchain pins and target), each program's maximum stack use, measured from the final binary on both backends, and the trusted-base statement naming the Lean kernel, LLVM printer and LLVM, LCB's MIR→assembly step, linker, OS and named runtime premises as not proven ([early decisions](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence)); the diagnostics and facts JSON schema is frozen and versioned from the first front-end output, with golden tests; a planted wrong fact and a planted unknown version are caught; Home: spec (the stack-bound claim and, from slice 0, the refusal of an unknown or over-budget bound against the `Concrete.toml` stack budget) and tooling (the report). Gate: stack, the final-artifact bound checked against the claim and the budget ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0499 |
ROADMAP.md:404:| R-0593 | The diagnostic and facts records, their frozen, versioned JSON schema, goldens and the parser's diagnostics from the first front-end output ([diagnostics decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-diagnostics-2026-11-06), [early decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence)) | every slice-0 refusal is one typed record rendered as human text and JSON lines (`--format=human|json`), with rule IDs from the Lean rule name and that rule's own revision (R-0603); the diagnostics and facts JSON schema is frozen and versioned from the first front-end output, and golden tests cover it and both diagnostic renderings; the LL(1) parser fills its expected-token set from the grammar data; B0's classified refusal controls use the records; Home: spec (the diagnostics and facts types are the schema source). Gate: the JSON schema generated from the Lean types, with goldens ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0592, R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:405:| R-0594 | Zero-heap facts proved in slice 0 ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-memory-2026-11-06)) | every slice-0 function's heap facts are 0, established by the derivation (no allocator is reachable), not by schema defaults; a planted reachable allocator flips them | R-0575, R-0592 |
ROADMAP.md:406:| R-0585 | Vacuity checks and contract-strength labels from the first proof slice | no fact reads "proved" when its theorem holds only under an unsatisfiable precondition or premise set, from the first proof slice on; the checker reports a context as infeasible wherever its octagon is empty, and a discharge inside an infeasible context is marked vacuous, never established, unless the infeasibility itself is the claim (a dead branch), applying OD120 from B1 rather than F; each proved claim carries its strength (a point instance, one direction, an equivalence or a full contract), so a single-input proof never reads as a universal contract; planted vacuous and point-only proofs are caught; obligation sufficiency stays with R-0460 and the phase-F validation project with R-0219 | R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:407:| R-0599 | Replayed counterexamples for "not established", without SMT | a deterministic bounded search evaluates a failed obligation's path constraints on boundary points (type extremes and the octagon's constants) and replays a candidate through the Lean reference evaluator with the exact semantics, inputs, resources and environment, satisfying the preconditions; a replayed witness reports "refuted, replayed", otherwise "no witness within N cases", never "safe"; the search never affects admission; due at B1 | R-0575, R-0586 |
ROADMAP.md:408:| R-0600 | A derived "always aborts" fact (SPARK `No_Return`) | a function whose every path aborts gets a derived fact, with no annotation and no function category (OD116); the facts report shows it, and the checker uses it to prune paths after a call to an abort wrapper within the decided divergence typing (TYPE-4@2); any change to the join rule goes to the owner; due with the functions-and-calls slice | R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:409:| R-0589 | Declared assumptions in `Concrete.toml`, with a change-detecting check ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-concrete-toml), 2026-11-06) | a project declares its target, heap, stack, foreign-function and trusted-surface assumptions in its `Concrete.toml` only where the facts cannot derive them; the section is typed, versioned data and an unknown key or version is refused; the facts query reports each assumption with the facts that rely on it; any change to the declared set is reported as an assumption diff and fails CI until the diff is recorded with the change; the sections are the typed, versioned `[policy]` and `[assumptions]` tables | R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:410:| R-0586 | The I/O driver for the Lean reference evaluator | the Lean evaluator runs programs that read and write their grants, so I/O examples get expected outcomes independent of the compiler; its infrastructure lands with slice 1 (B1), and from slice 16 (the console) it runs every supported I/O example and produces its expected observations independently of the compiler, and R-0491 uses them | R-0496, R-0425 |
ROADMAP.md:411:| R-0576 | The predictable policy ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-predictable-policy), 2026-11-06) | an optional module or project policy, built on the per-function facts (R-0575), refuses at compile time any function in its scope that allocates, blocks or calls foreign code, and, when the policy demands it, any function without termination evidence or a stack bound; admission requires established evidence of no allocation, no blocking and no foreign calls, so an unknown or incomplete fact is refused; the policy explicitly states whether named trusted assumptions are acceptable (Q13); a violation names the function, the fact and its source; the query reports which policy covers each function; a planted violation and a planted unknown fact are both refused; the policy is declared in the project's `Concrete.toml` ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-concrete-toml)) as typed, versioned data, a module may tighten it but never relax it, and dependencies are checked against the consumer's policy transitively and fail-closed; Home: tooling (the optional `Concrete.toml` policy over the facts; the mandatory slice-0 stack-budget refusal is R-0575's). Gate: the policy's refusal controls ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:412:| R-0577 | Reproducible builds checked in CI from slice 0 ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-reproducible-builds), 2026-11-06, revised 2026-11-07) | the same source, compiler revision, toolchain pin and target give byte-identical BIR, LLVM IR text, LCB job and output, and final binary; pins cover the nix flake, LCB revision and LLVM/clang version; artifacts contain no timestamps, absolute paths or host names (`-ffile-prefix-map`, `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`, deterministic link order), and output never depends on hash-map iteration order; CI builds every slice-0 example twice in different directories and on a GitHub runner and concrete-ci-2 and compares SHA-256, failing on any difference; `concrete facts` carries the compiler SHA, toolchain pins and target; work started on `wip/slice0-repro`; Home: tooling (build pins and CI). Gate: reproducibility, every slice-0 example built in two directories and on two machines (a GitHub runner and concrete-ci-2), SHA-256 compared ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0503 |
ROADMAP.md:413:| R-0479 | Failures and refusals are never turned into facts; diagnostics name the valid next action ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-diagnostics-2026-11-06)) | refusals survive through results, text, JSON and exit codes; fixes are offered only rechecked and labelled, and applied automatically only when a lemma proves them meaning-preserving; there are no warnings | R-0499 |
ROADMAP.md:414:| R-0118 | Generated coverage of passes, consumers, constructs, operations and traps | a missing row or a synthetic constructor fails the gate; B0 includes pipeline-edge coverage and controls for missing proofs, unregistered premises, wrong artifact substitution and reject-all compilation | R-0501 |
ROADMAP.md:415:| R-0491 | The defect inventory and the classified differential (Lean semantics, native build, legacy oracle) | comparisons are classified and campaigns are non-vacuous; its check refuses any expected outcome recorded from compiler output (I/O expectations come from the Lean evaluator's driver, R-0586) | R-0425, R-0564, R-0586 |
ROADMAP.md:416:| R-0498 | Every enforcement claim maps to its gate leg and a targeted mutation | an unmapped claim fails; shape checks are labelled unproved; B0 includes pipeline-edge coverage and controls for missing proofs, unregistered premises, wrong artifact substitution and reject-all compilation | R-0118 |
ROADMAP.md:417:| R-0272 | Restart fuzzing against the new compiler on concrete-ci-2, including the random-program differential against the Lean evaluator from the slice-0 front end ([early decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence)), seeded from the regressions and examples that survive in the repository and from newly generated programs (the ci-3 corpus and findings were not copied before the handover, decision row P14, and are lost), with reduction and counterexample-to-regression persistence | the random-program differential starts with the slice-0 front end and grows one feature per slice; the generators emit slice-supported programs, the oracle is Lean semantics against native code, and findings become regressions; legacy fuzzing stopped at the move; Home: tooling. Gate: the fuzzer, short in CI and long nightly on concrete-ci-2 ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0425 |
ROADMAP.md:418:| R-0550 | Compiler speed, scaling and binary-size tracking from slice 0 ([speed decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-performance), [early decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence)) | wall time, per-stage time and memory are recorded by exact SHA, backend and target from slice 0; binary size is tracked per example per commit next to speed tracking and is report-only; speed measurements are report-only until their noise is understood, then scaling gates hold for each stage and a planted regression is caught; Home: tooling. Gate: speed and binary size, report-only ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0503 |
